Bug Report: Incorrect JSON Schema Validation for notebook.cell.chat.start Keyboard Shortcuts

Product: Visual Studio Code
Version: 1.144.0
Operating System: Windows 11
Component: Notebook / Keyboard Shortcuts / JSON Schema Validation

Description

The built-in keyboard shortcuts Ctrl+I and Ctrl+K I, associated with the command notebook.cell.chat.start, produce yellow validation warnings in the virtual editor Default Keyboard Shortcuts (JSON).

The warning displayed by VS Code is:

Missing property: "args"

The issue persists after upgrading VS Code to version 1.144.0.

Affected shortcuts
json
{
    "key": "ctrl+k i",
    "command": "notebook.cell.chat.start",
    "when": "config.notebook.experimental.cellChat && notebookChatAgentRegistered && notebookEditable && notebookEditorFocused && !inputFocus || config.notebook.experimental.generate && notebookChatAgentRegistered && notebookEditable && notebookEditorFocused && !inputFocus"
},
{
    "key": "ctrl+i",
    "command": "notebook.cell.chat.start",
    "when": "config.notebook.experimental.cellChat && notebookChatAgentRegistered && notebookEditable && notebookEditorFocused && !inputFocus || config.notebook.experimental.generate && notebookChatAgentRegistered && notebookEditable && notebookEditorFocused && !inputFocus"
}

Relevant source code

File:

src/vs/workbench/contrib/notebook/browser/controller/chat/cellChatActions.ts

The command metadata defines:

ts
args: [
    {
        name: 'args',
        schema: {
            type: 'object',
            required: ['index'],
            properties: {
                index: {
                    type: 'number'
                },
                input: {
                    type: 'string'
                },
                autoSend: {
                    type: 'boolean'
                }
            }
        }
    }
]


However, the built-in keyboard shortcuts do not supply an args property.

Furthermore, the command implementation explicitly handles the absence of arguments by retrieving the active notebook cell.

Schema generation

File:

src/vs/workbench/services/keybinding/browser/keybindingService.ts

The schema generation logic determines whether args is mandatory using isOptional.

When isOptional is undefined, the implementation falls back to checking whether the argument schema contains required properties.

Because index is required, the generated keybindings JSON schema also requires args.

This produces a validation warning for the built-in shortcuts, despite the command implementation supporting calls without arguments.

Expected behavior

The built-in shortcuts should not generate JSON schema validation warnings when their command implementation supports execution without arguments.

The schema should accurately reflect the actual command behavior.

Suggested correction

Consider adding:

ts
isOptional: true,


to the argument metadata:

ts
args: [
    {
        name: 'args',
        isOptional: true,
        schema: {
            type: 'object',
            required: ['index'],
            properties: {
                index: {
                    type: 'number'
                },
                input: {
                    type: 'string'
                },
                autoSend: {
                    type: 'boolean'
                }
            }
        }
    }
]


This would make the outer args property optional while preserving the requirement for index whenever an argument object is supplied.

Additional observations
The command is recognized by VS Code.
The warning also appears in the virtual default keybindings editor.
Adding args: { "index": 0 } to the user keybindings file does not permanently eliminate the warnings in the virtual editor.
Saving the user keybindings file temporarily clears the warnings, but they subsequently reappear.
Restarting VS Code does not resolve the issue.
The original compiled JavaScript installation files have not been modified.
Request

Please investigate the inconsistency between the command metadata, the generated keybindings JSON schema and the actual command implementation.

The schema should not require arguments that the built-in keyboard shortcuts intentionally omit and that the command implementation explicitly supports omitting.

Reported with technical analysis assistance from ChatGPT.
